!! Two protocols, one wiki

`--mcp` and `--lsp` are separate commands. They answer from the same wiki in one of two ways.

!!! One process

Both commands on one command line run against the same wiki store:

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --mcp rw listen --lsp
```

A tiddler an assistant creates over MCP is a title your editor completes moments later, and a tiddler it renames stops being offered.

!!! The editor's own process

When the editor starts the LSP server (see [[LSP Configuration]]), that process boots its own copy of the wiki, so the MCP server can stop and start without taking the editor features down. The two copies are kept in step:

* The LSP server reads in tiddler files the MCP server writes for MCP tools and browser edits, as they change on disk. A file that was only read is left alone.
* A `.tid` saved in the editor is reloaded into the MCP server too, so the browser and MCP tools see the save.
* Hovers link shadow tiddlers to the MCP server's browser port, which it records in `.tw-mcp/connect`.

The LSP server finds the MCP server through `.tw-mcp/connect`, keeps a connection to it over the MCP pipe, and says who it is. Each side logs the other by label:

```
[tw-lsp @lsp-mcp-server] MCP server found: PID 22696 @sse-primary, browser on port 8888
[tw-mcp 10:23:03.412] LSP client: Visual Studio Code 1.123.0 (PID 12388, pipe) @lsp-mcp-server on /home/me/tw-mcp-server
```

`get_wiki_info` lists the LSP servers of the wiki, and the editor's status bar shows `lsp-mcp-server ⇄ sse-primary`. When the MCP server stops, the LSP log says `MCP server gone` and the status bar drops the second label. Try it in [[LSP and MCP - (Example)]].

Neither command depends on the other: what they share is code. The editor's [[find references|LSP Find References]] and the [[find_calls]] MCP tool read call sites from the same library, and the editor's [[go to definition|LSP Go to Definition]] and the [[find_definition]] MCP tool find the running ~JavaScript through the same module. So each works alone, and together an assistant and your editor agree on every call and definition.

!!! Why they are not one command

The two protocols disagree below the wiki. MCP `2026-07-28` is stateless: every message carries its own version and identity, and the server keeps nothing per connection. LSP is stateful by construction: it negotiates capabilities once, mirrors every buffer the editor opens, and stops answering after `shutdown`.

Folding that lifecycle into `--mcp` would either erode its stateless contract, or grow a second stack beside it that shares nothing but the wiki.

!!! The one thing they cannot share

Framing. MCP over stdio separates messages with newlines, while LSP counts bytes in a `Content-Length` header. One stdin cannot carry both, so `--lsp stdio` and `--mcp` refuse to start together, whichever you list first:

```
Error: --lsp stdio cannot run beside --mcp: both would read the same pipe, and their framings differ. Use --lsp port=<n> instead.
```

Give the LSP server a port and the two coexist.
